<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    looptest: where a bottom-tested loop prints its test.

    Issue: kuna-looptest (decbench O2: gcc's loop header copying turns
    `while (c) body` into `if (c) do body while (c)`, and kuna printed the
    rotated form).  Stage P8 (goto-quality), P0 assertion
    `option looptest binary|proven|assume`.

    Authored x86-64 (nasm), each function laid out as gcc -O2 emits it:
      fill   if (n == 0) return; end = a + n; do *a++ = v; while (a != end);
             (the guard's return and the loop's return are two blocks)
      drain  if (n > 0) { i = 0; do { a[i] = i; i++; } while (i < n); } return n;
      zero16 i = 0; do { a[i] = 0; i++; } while (i != 16);   (no guard)
      count  if (n > 0) { i = 0; do ...; while (i != n); }   (i steps by 1 from 0
             to n, so i != n is i < n, the guard's test)
      walk   if (p) do { q = *p; p[1] = 0; p = q; } while (q);
      skip   do c = *s++; while (c != ' ');   (the test reads a body value)

    Pass 1 (default `proven`): fill, drain and walk print `while (c)` with
    the guard gone, zero16 prints `while` because its entry test folds to
    true, walk's test reads `p` (the variable `q` is copied into), count
    prints `while (i < n)` and skip stays do-while.  Pass 2 (`option looptest binary`): every loop is
    a do-while again and fill, drain and walk keep their guards.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="fill"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100040" name="drain"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100080" name="zero16"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000c0" name="count"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100100" name="walk"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100140" name="skip"/>
</binaryimage>
